  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on outcomes with probabilities below 20%, which are reweighted by increasing their probability by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, the outcome of -4000 Euros has a 20% probability—this is not below 20%, so it would not be reweighted. In Lottery B, the outcome of -3000 Euros has a 25% probability, which is above 20%, so it also would not be reweighted. Thus, no outcome in either lottery falls below the 20% threshold, meaning no probability is adjusted. The reason would then default to standard expected value calculation for the 'small probability' components (which are none), or be indifferent if only considering this specific adjustment. However, the rule implies a focus on rare events (below 20%), and since no outcome qualifies, the reason may not favor either lottery based on its reweighting rule.",
